    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3f88fe40",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a police officer kneeling and interacting with a child on a bicycle, with overlaid text reading \"I'm arresting you for public intoxication.\" This juxtaposition is clearly absurd and satirical, as a child cannot be intoxicated in the legal sense. The intent is to create humor through the ridiculousness of the situation, implying the child is being arrested for something trivial or nonsensical. While not targeting any specific group with hate or abuse, the meme's harmful potential lies in its potential to normalize or trivialize law enforcement actions, especially when applied to children, which could be seen as undermining trust in authority or promoting a cynical view of policing. The humor is derived from the incongruity between the serious tone of the text and the innocent, non-threatening context of the image, making it a harmless joke rather than a hateful or abusive one.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0c1a35c3"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
